\subsection*{\color{human}Turn 001 --- Human}

The MRVA test sequence is predefined:

\[
  \text{setup}
  \longrightarrow \text{pick database}
  \longrightarrow \text{pick query}
  \longrightarrow \text{submit job}
  \longrightarrow \text{collect results}.
\]

Interactive use requires:

\noindent\textbullet\ manual database creation or selection;

\noindent\textbullet\ manual query development and refinement.

Thus, two of the original steps become multi-step activities. How should this
change be represented:

\noindent\textbullet\ in the script;

\noindent\textbullet\ in the UI;

\noindent\textbullet\ for LLM assistance?

% === TURN 001 | HUMAN | END ===

% === TURN 002 | LLM | BEGIN ===
\subsection*{\color{llm}Turn 002 --- LLM}

Yes. I would preserve the five-step semantic sequence and change the
cardinality of two steps, rather than turning the whole system into a longer
sequence.

Let the artifact-producing portion of the sequence be

\[
  S = D \longrightarrow Q \longrightarrow J \longrightarrow R,
\]

where setup precedes \(S\). In the predefined test,

\[
  D = \operatorname{pick\text{-}db},
  \qquad
  Q = \operatorname{pick\text{-}query}.
\]

Interactive use changes those two selections into workspaces or refinement
loops:

\[
  D^{*} \longrightarrow Q^{*} \longrightarrow J \longrightarrow R,
\]

with

\[
  D^{*}
  =
  \bigl(
    \text{create}
    \mid \text{choose}
    \mid \text{inspect}
    \mid \text{repair}
  \bigr)^{*}
  \longrightarrow D,
\]

and

\[
  Q^{*}
  =
  \bigl(
    \text{create}
    \mid \text{edit}
    \mid \text{run}
    \mid \text{inspect}
  \bigr)^{*}
  \longrightarrow Q.
\]

The important invariant is that both loops eventually produce exactly the same
objects consumed by the predefined test:

\[
  D^{*} \longrightarrow D,
  \qquad
  Q^{*} \longrightarrow Q.
\]

Therefore, I would not change the downstream MRVA model.

\subsubsection*{Script}

The script should reflect artifact production, not every interactive
operation:

\begin{verbatim}
setup
db           -> D
query        -> Q
submit D Q   -> J
collect J    -> R
\end{verbatim}

The database and query stages each have two possible realizations:

\begin{verbatim}
db:
    predefined -> choose existing D
    interactive -> work ... -> D

query:
    predefined -> choose existing Q
    interactive -> work ... -> Q
\end{verbatim}

This is cleaner than promoting \texttt{create-db}, \texttt{edit-query},
\texttt{test-query}, and similar operations to peers of \texttt{submit}.
Those are operations inside a stage.

\subsubsection*{UI}

The UI can distinguish pipeline stages from stage internals:

\begin{verbatim}
[ setup ] -> [ database ] -> [ query ] -> [ submit ] -> [ results ]
                  |              |
                  v              v
             DB workspace   Query workspace
             ------------   ---------------
             choose         choose
             create         edit
             build          run locally
             inspect        inspect results
             repair         refine
                  |              |
                  +-> D           +-> Q
\end{verbatim}

This fits the existing top-view model: the top-level bar does not grow.
Selecting the database or query stage exposes its richer internal state
machine.

Thus,

\[
  \text{pipeline topology}
  \neq
  \text{interaction topology}.
\]

The pipeline remains simple; complexity is encapsulated inside its stages.

\subsubsection*{LLM assistance}

This representation is also useful for an LLM because the stage boundary is
explicit:

\[
  \text{goal: produce a valid } D,
  \qquad
  \text{then produce a satisfactory } Q.
\]

The LLM does not need a prescribed workflow. It observes the current stage
state and acts until the stage's output predicate holds:

\[
  P_D(D) = \mathrm{ready},
  \qquad
  P_Q(Q,D) = \mathrm{ready}.
\]

For example, query development naturally becomes

\[
\begin{aligned}
  Q_0
  &\longrightarrow \operatorname{run}(Q_0,D)
   \longrightarrow \operatorname{inspect}(R_0) \\
  &\longrightarrow Q_1
   \longrightarrow \operatorname{run}(Q_1,D)
   \longrightarrow \operatorname{inspect}(R_1) \\
  &\longrightarrow \cdots
   \longrightarrow Q_n,
\end{aligned}
\]

followed by

\[
  Q_n \xrightarrow{\mathrm{accept}} Q.
\]

This is closer to what an LLM is good at than encoding a fixed
\texttt{develop-query} workflow. The loop need not exist as an explicit
workflow; it is repeated state transformation under a goal predicate.

I would use one abstraction consistently across all three representations:

\[
  \text{stage}
  =
  \text{arbitrary work}
  \longrightarrow
  \text{artifact satisfying an exit predicate}.
\]

The predefined test is the degenerate case in which the arbitrary work has one
operation:

\[
  D^{*} = \operatorname{pick}(D),
  \qquad
  Q^{*} = \operatorname{pick}(Q),
\]

while interactive CGA exposes the full \(D^{*}\) and \(Q^{*}\) spaces.

This preserves the useful property that the predefined test and the
interactive system are structurally the same system, rather than a ``test
workflow'' and a separate ``real workflow.''

% === TURN 002 | LLM | END ===

