\subsection*{\color{human}Turn 003 --- Human}

That is very good. There is one additional repetition: \(S\) itself.

\[
  S_1 = D_1^{*} \longrightarrow \cdots,
  \qquad
  S_2 = D_2^{*} \longrightarrow \cdots,
  \qquad
  \ldots
\]

The repeated sessions are followed by comparison across the \(R_i\).

% === TURN 003 | HUMAN | END ===

% === TURN 004 | LLM | BEGIN ===
\subsection*{\color{llm}Turn 004 --- LLM}

Yes. That makes the structure cleaner: there are three nested levels of
iteration, not a longer pipeline.

For each session,

\[
  S_i
  =
  D_i^{*}
  \longrightarrow Q_i^{*}
  \longrightarrow J_i
  \longrightarrow R_i,
\]

with

\[
  D_i^{*} \longrightarrow D_i,
  \qquad
  Q_i^{*}(D_i) \longrightarrow Q_i.
\]

The experiment is then

\[
  E
  =
  \{S_1,S_2,\ldots,S_n\}
  \longrightarrow
  C(R_1,R_2,\ldots,R_n),
\]

where \(C\) is comparison or evaluation.

The complete topology is approximately:

\begin{verbatim}
Experiment E

    S1: D1* -> Q1* -> J1 -> R1 --+
                                   |
    S2: D2* -> Q2* -> J2 -> R2 --+--> C(R1,...,Rn)
                                   |
     ...                           |
                                   |
    Sn: Dn* -> Qn* -> Jn -> Rn --+
\end{verbatim}

The three repetitions have different meanings:

\[
\begin{aligned}
  D_i^{*} &: \text{construction and refinement of a database}, \\
  Q_i^{*} &: \text{construction and refinement of a query}, \\
  S^{*}   &: \text{production of comparable observations}.
\end{aligned}
\]

I would therefore resist representing all three as generic loops.

\subsubsection*{Script}

The natural script abstraction becomes:

\begin{verbatim}
experiment
    session S1
        db      -> D1
        query   -> Q1
        submit  -> J1
        collect -> R1

    session S2
        db      -> D2
        query   -> Q2
        submit  -> J2
        collect -> R2

    compare R1 R2 ...
\end{verbatim}

The session is now a first-class object, matching the existing MRVA session
concept. More formally,

\[
  S_i = (D_i,Q_i,J_i,R_i).
\]

Comparison can therefore operate on sessions rather than manually assembled
result sets:

\[
  C(S_1,\ldots,S_n),
  \qquad
  R(S_i) = R_i.
\]

This becomes useful when comparison depends on provenance---query version,
database, timestamps, or configuration---rather than only SARIF.

\subsubsection*{UI}

The highest-level UI object should perhaps be the experiment rather than the
pipeline:

\begin{verbatim}
Experiment
|
+-- S1
|   +-- database
|   +-- query
|   +-- job
|   `-- results
|
+-- S2
|   +-- database
|   +-- query
|   +-- job
|   `-- results
|
+-- ...
|
`-- comparison
\end{verbatim}

Selecting \(S_i\) reveals the existing horizontal pipeline:

\[
  D_i \longrightarrow Q_i \longrightarrow J_i \longrightarrow R_i.
\]

Selecting the database or query opens its refinement space. The UI therefore
has three scales:

\[
  E
  \supset
  S_i
  \supset
  \{D_i^{*},Q_i^{*},J_i,R_i\}.
\]

This is close to the observation that both the UI and the underlying flow are
trees.

\subsubsection*{LLM assistance}

The hierarchy gives the LLM three clean goals:

\[
\begin{aligned}
  \text{local goal}      &: \text{produce } D_i \text{ or } Q_i, \\
  \text{session goal}    &: \text{produce } R_i, \\
  \text{experiment goal} &: \text{produce } C(R_1,\ldots,R_n).
\end{aligned}
\]

The LLM need not be told which level of iteration it is performing; the
current goal and state determine that. For example,

\[
  Q_i^{0} \longrightarrow Q_i^{1} \longrightarrow Q_i^{2}
\]

is refinement within \(S_i\), whereas

\[
  S_i \longrightarrow S_{i+1}
\]

means preserving the previous observation and constructing another
observation for comparison.

This distinction gives a clean immutability boundary:

\[
  S_i\ \mathrm{complete}
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  S_i\ \text{is evidence, not workspace}.
\]

If the query changes afterward, do not mutate \(S_i\):

\[
  Q_i \longrightarrow Q'
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  S_{i+1} = (D',Q',J',R').
\]

The interactive process becomes an accumulating experiment:

\[
  E_k = \{S_1,\ldots,S_k\},
  \qquad
  E_k \subset E_{k+1}.
\]

That fits the immutable, accumulating-state model already established for CGA.

