\subsection*{\color{human}Turn 005 --- Human}

The structure is great. Do you have suggestions for UI interaction? One option
is to try \(S_i\), then pause when incomplete user input is required.

% === TURN 005 | HUMAN | END ===

% === TURN 006 | LLM | BEGIN ===
\subsection*{\color{llm}Turn 006 --- LLM}

Yes. ``Run \(S_i\) until blocked'' is probably the right primitive. It gives
one interaction model for scripted, manual, and LLM-driven use.

Define execution as

\[
  \operatorname{advance}(S_i)
  \longrightarrow
  \begin{cases}
    \operatorname{advance}(S_i'),
      & \text{an automatic step is possible}, \\
    \operatorname{wait}(S_i,q),
      & \text{input is required}, \\
    \operatorname{done}(S_i,R_i),
      & \text{the session is complete}.
  \end{cases}
\]

The UI is not navigating a wizard; it is displaying the current state of an
executing \(S_i\).

\subsubsection*{Interaction}

Suppose a new session begins:

\begin{verbatim}
S3

  database    [ ]
  query       [ ]
  submit      [ ]
  results     [ ]
\end{verbatim}

Advancing immediately reaches database selection:

\begin{verbatim}
S3

  database    [?]  [ choose | create ]
  query       [ ]
  submit      [ ]
  results     [ ]
\end{verbatim}

If the user chooses \texttt{create}, that node expands into its workspace.
When \(D_3\) becomes valid:

\begin{verbatim}
  database    [x] D3
  query       [?]  [ choose | develop ]
  submit      [ ]
  results     [ ]
\end{verbatim}

Developing a query similarly expands the query workspace:

\begin{verbatim}
  database    [x] D3
  query       [...]
      Q0 -> run -> inspect
      Q1 -> run -> inspect
      Q2
               [ accept ]
  submit      [ ]
  results     [ ]
\end{verbatim}

Acceptance establishes

\[
  Q_3 := Q_2,
\]

and execution resumes automatically:

\begin{verbatim}
  database    [x] D3
  query       [x] Q3
  submit      [...]
  results     [ ]
\end{verbatim}

Pause and resume should therefore be implicit:

\[
  \mathrm{blocked} \equiv \mathrm{paused},
  \qquad
  \text{required input supplied}
  \Longrightarrow
  \operatorname{advance}.
\]

\subsubsection*{Do not distinguish human and LLM input}

A blocked state should describe the missing information, not who should supply
it:

\[
  B = (S_i,\ \text{requirement},\ \text{available actions}).
\]

For example,

\[
  B_Q
  =
  \bigl(
    S_3,\,
    \text{need accepted query},\,
    \{\text{choose},\text{edit},\text{run},\text{accept}\}
  \bigr).
\]

Then:

\begin{verbatim}
                 +-- human
                 |
S_i -> BLOCKED --+-- LLM
                 |
                 `-- script
                       |
                       v
                    state'
                       |
                       v
                    advance
\end{verbatim}

LLM assistance becomes an alternative producer of state transitions, rather
than a special LLM mode.

\subsubsection*{UI consequence}

Keep the top-level UI stable:

\begin{verbatim}
Experiment E
----------------------------------------

S1   [x]   D1 -> Q1 -> J1 -> R1
S2   [x]   D2 -> Q2 -> J2 -> R2
S3   [?]   D3 -> Q3 ...
S4   [ ]

----------------------------------------
Compare: S1 S2
\end{verbatim}

Selecting \(S_3\) reveals its pipeline. Selecting the blocked node reveals its
workspace. This gives three zoom levels without three UI paradigms:

\[
  E
  \xrightarrow{\text{select }S_i}
  S_i
  \xrightarrow{\text{select stage}}
  \text{workspace}.
\]

Make the current blockage visually dominant. The user should not need to ask
what to do next:

\[
  \operatorname{next}(E)
  =
  \text{first unresolved requirement of the active } S_i.
\]

\subsubsection*{Do not force session completion}

The user may discover while developing \(Q_i\) that another experiment is
useful. Permit

\[
  S_i : \mathrm{blocked/incomplete}
\]

while creating \(S_{i+1}\). Then \(E\) is genuinely a collection of
independent sessions:

\[
  E
  =
  \left\{
    S_i
    \,\middle|\,
    \operatorname{state}(S_i)
    \in
    \{\mathrm{incomplete},\mathrm{running},\mathrm{complete}\}
  \right\}.
\]

That is better than a wizard. The default interaction can still advance until
blocked, but blockage does not capture the UI; it identifies the currently
unresolved edge.

The central UI operation can therefore be stated literally as

\[
  \operatorname{advance}(S_i)
  \quad
  \text{until blocked or complete}.
\]

Everything else---manual work, LLM assistance, and scripts---is machinery for
resolving the block.

